    "current_priorities": [
      "Trust first: reduce startup/plugin weirdness, make degraded-mode behavior explain itself, improve bulk-edit confidence (`replace`/`replaceall`/`qreplace` previews and clearer progress), and keep file/save/readonly/encoding behavior visibly honest. The repo already has a lot of the right substrate \u2014 capability gates, inspectable status/prompt/screen models, grouped plugin cleanup, readonly/protected-buffer paths, and portability discipline \u2014 so the next wins should mostly make that existing safety model feel human rather than implicit.",
      "Taste second: keep iterating on picker/TUI readability, but now as one coherent visual system rather than a pile of isolated cues. The editor already has a strong inventory of tiny affordances (`hlsearch`, `cursorline`, `ruler`, `relativeruler`, `scrollmargin`, `hltrailingws`, `hltaberrors`, `colorcolumn`, `scrollbar`, `matchbrace`, overflow markers, `$(bufpos)`, `keymenu`, `infobar`, `constantshow`, `screen_model(...)`, `display_rows_model(...)`, `viewport_cues_model(...)`, and the shared prompt/status/panel/layout models); the next job is to curate defaults, previews, and hierarchy so the surface feels designed rather than merely featureful.",
      "Flow third: keep making navigation and edit loops cheaper once trust/taste are sturdier. That means better file/project movement, better jump/return/orientation loops, continued picker scan-speed improvements, safer/faster repetitive editing, and later multi-buffer/split-aware workflows once the single-window interaction model feels settled.",
      "Docs browser polish: keep adding small markdown affordances only when they improve real docs usage or sharpen the shared inspectable docs/help models. That lane stays valuable, but it should compete against taste/trust/flow rather than automatically outranking them.",
      "Portability discipline: keep expanding the portable corpus / cross-host validation story as the VM surface grows, because long-term trust in Micromax as a language/runtime still depends on replayable semantics across future non-Python hosts too.",
      "Safety boundaries: keep expanding capability-gated surfaces (jobs, filesystem, UI integration) while keeping defaults safe and degraded modes explicit."
    ],
